  [1mThe original decision[0m ([38;2;177;185;249mdecision-456d94e3[39m, "SQLite, single file
  next to the app") solved the problem it was made for. The
  JSON file lost writes when two requests arrived at once.
  SQLite gives transactional writes, and it avoided running a
  database server, which is why Postgres was turned down as
  overkill for a one-person app. Nothing in the log says SQLite
  failed at that job.

  [1mWhy[0m [1mit[0m [1mwas[0m [1msuperseded:[0m a later decision ([38;2;177;185;249mdecision-370866d3[39m)
  says the requirements changed. You now want to use pantry from
  both your phone and your laptop, and a single SQLite file
  next to the app can't be shared between devices. It proposes
  Postgres on your home server instead. It accepts the cost the
  SQLite decision was avoiding, a separate server to run and
  back up, and notes that transactional writes are still
  covered.

  [1mWhere[0m [1mthings[0m [1mstand:[0m
  - The Postgres decision is only [1mproposed[0m, not accepted. It was
    logged by a Codex agent, not in a Claude session.
  - The code hasn't moved. [38;2;177;185;249mpantry/store.py[39m still writes to
    [38;2;177;185;249mpantry.db[39m with SQLite, and the latest commit is the SQLite
    migration ([38;2;177;185;249m8443c86[39m). There's no Postgres code and no
    connection config.
  - I tried to fetch the formal outcome record for the SQLite
    decision, but hivemind's [38;2;177;185;249mget_decision_outcome[39m returned a
    graph query error. So this summary comes from the two
    decisions themselves, not from its derived quality signals.

  So SQLite was the right fix for lost writes; it was your
  requirements that changed. If the phone-and-laptop use is
  real, the next step is to accept the Postgres decision and
  migrate [38;2;177;185;249mstore.py[39m. One thing to settle first: if the phone only
  reaches the app over HTTP on the same machine, SQLite would
  still work, because only the server touches the file. Postgres
  only becomes necessary if more than one app instance or
  machine has to write to the data. Do you want me to check how
  the phone will connect before committing to Postgres?

